<BaseLayout
  title="Jeddah vs Dammam — Which Saudi Port Should Your China Freight Use? (August 2026)"
  description="Jeddah vs Dammam port comparison for China freight: Red Sea vs Arabian Gulf location, transit windows, cost differences, hinterland decision framework, cargo fit, and SABER/VAT compliance."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Jeddah vs Dammam' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">August 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Jeddah vs Dammam: which Saudi port should your China freight use?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The definitive China–Saudi port-routing guide. This page compares Jeddah Islamic Port on the
        Red Sea with King Abdulaziz Port Dammam on the Arabian Gulf, then gives the hinterland
        decision framework most forwarders skip: western-KSA cargo vs Eastern Province cargo vs
        Riyadh cargo vs oil &amp; gas project cargo vs religious goods.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative market and port snapshots. Treat
        every LOW-confidence transit, facility and fee note as indicative, verify with Mawani or your
        carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Saudi import VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">15% <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">SABER SC rule</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Before arrival <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Jeddah sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">~18 days typical <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Dammam sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">~20 days typical <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Riyadh via Dammam</span>
          <span class="fact-value">30–45 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Mawani total</span>
          <span class="fact-value">8.3M TEU (2025) <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why the Jeddah vs Dammam decision matters</h2>
    <p>
      Jeddah and Dammam are not competing on price the way two terminals in the same bay might.
      They sit on opposite coasts, serve different hinterlands, and lead to very different inland
      legs once the container is off the vessel. That is why the question “which Saudi port should I
      use?” is almost always an <em>inland-leg decision</em> disguised as a routing question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Jeddah Islamic Port is the Red Sea gateway for western Saudi Arabia: Jeddah city, Makkah,
      Madinah, Tabuk and the religious and Red Sea trade that runs through them. King Abdulaziz Port
      Dammam is the Arabian Gulf gateway for the Eastern Province — the country’s oil and gas
      heartland — and the established entry point for Riyadh through the Riyadh Dry Port rail/road
      corridor. Choosing the wrong coast does not just add distance; it adds an extra inland move,
      another handover point, and often a demurrage or detention exposure if the documents are not
      ready in time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The verified research snapshot does not publish port-specific throughput for either Jeddah or
      Dammam. It does confirm the Mawani total: Saudi ports handled <strong>8.3 million TEU in
      2025</strong>, up 10.6% year-on-year. Port-specific berth, depth and TEU figures are therefore
      marked LOW and attributed to the Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani) rather than invented.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Jeddah vs Dammam at a glance</h2>
      <p>
        Use this side-by-side table as the orientation before you get into the cargo-specific decision
        framework below. The coastal position and hinterland are the rows that matter most; the
        port-specific capacity rows stay empty of invented numbers because the verified snapshot did
        not publish them.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'parameter', label: 'Parameter' },
          { key: 'jeddah', label: 'Jeddah' },
          { key: 'dammam', label: 'Dammam' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={comparisonRows}
        caption="Side-by-side comparison — verify berth, depth and per-port throughput with the Saudi Ports Authority (Mawani) before relying on these details."
      />
      <SourceList sources={portSources} title="Sources — Jeddah & Dammam port parameters" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. China to Jeddah vs Dammam: transit windows</h2>
    <p>
      At the China end, the corridor is fed by some of the world’s largest export gateways: Shanghai
      handled <strong>55.06 million TEU in 2025</strong> and Ningbo-Zhoushan handled
      <strong>43 million TEU</strong>, both HIGH-confidence figures from port-authority reporting. In
      practice, that means both Saudi gateways draw on deep, frequent sailing capacity from the main
      Chinese load ports.
    </p>
    <p>
      Transit to Jeddah and Dammam is close at the port-to-port level, and the real difference appears
      when cargo continues inland. Treat the figures below as planning ranges, not promises, and
      confirm the vessel, voyage, transhipment points and inland handover before committing a delivery
      date.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'origin', label: 'Origin' },
        { key: 'destination', label: 'Destination' },
        { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={transitRows}
      caption="Indicative transit windows only — verify with the relevant carrier before relying on these figures."
    />
    <SourceList sources={transitSources} title="Sources — China to Jeddah / Dammam transit times" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Port-to-port vs Riyadh door:</strong> about 18–20 days typical for the ocean leg is not
      the same as total door time to Riyadh. Add the Dammam discharge, rail/road transfer and final
      trucking; Freightos and market context put the Shanghai–Riyadh corridor around 30–45 days.
    </div>

    <SourceList sources={chinaOriginSources} title="Sources — China origin-port context" />
