  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. The hinterland decision framework: which cargo goes where</h2>
      <p>
        This is the core of the page, and the part competitors usually leave out. Rather than asking
        “which port is better”, ask “where is the consignee, and what kind of cargo is it?” The table
        below converts that question into a routing rule, and the sub-sections that follow explain the
        four cargo families in detail.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'cargo', label: 'Destination / cargo type' },
          { key: 'bestPort', label: 'Best-fit port' },
          { key: 'why', label: 'Why' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={decisionRows}
        caption="Hinterland routing heuristics, not guaranteed carrier schedules. Confirm the inland leg and cost before choosing the discharge port."
      />
      <SourceList sources={decisionSources} title="Sources — Jeddah vs Dammam routing context" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">Western-KSA and religious cargo → Jeddah</h3>
      <p>
        If the goods are destined for Jeddah city, Makkah, Madinah, Tabuk or the surrounding western
        provinces, Jeddah is the obvious gate. It keeps the final trucking leg short and puts the
        container inside the region it is meant to serve. The same logic applies to religious and
        Hajj/Umrah-related freight: Jeddah is the nearest major container gateway to the two holy
        cities, so pilgrimage-season cargo, mosque fixtures, hospitality supplies and consumer goods
        for the western corridor all naturally lean to the Red Sea side.
      </p>

      <h3>Eastern Province cargo → Dammam</h3>
      <p>
        Dammam is inside the Eastern Province industrial corridor, close to Dammam, Khobar, Jubail and
        the energy and petrochemical activity around them. A consignee in that region gains nothing by
        routing through Jeddah and then trucking across the country; the Arabian Gulf discharge is the
        shortest, cheapest and most operationally predictable flow for eastern-KSA deliveries.
      </p>

      <h3>Riyadh and central Saudi Arabia → Dammam + Riyadh Dry Port</h3>
      <p>
        Riyadh is the special case that surprises first-time shippers. Even though Jeddah is a major
        gateway, the conventional freight corridor into the capital runs through Dammam and Riyadh Dry
        Port. The container discharges at Dammam, moves inland by rail or road, and clears close to the
        capital for final distribution. That inland flow is the reason the decision framework treats
        “Riyadh” as a Dammam answer, not a Jeddah answer, unless a forwarder can demonstrate a better
        door-to-door case on the western route.
      </p>

      <h3>Oil &amp; gas equipment and project cargo → Dammam</h3>
      <p>
        Oversized and heavy energy equipment belongs on the Arabian Gulf side. Dammam sits closest to
        the Eastern Province oil and gas sites and project-cargo corridors, and it is the established
        gateway for industrial machinery, drilling equipment, pipe and heavy breakbulk. For these
        moves, lift capacity, laydown area and route clearances matter more than the container rate, so
        confirm those before booking and treat any “one port is always better” claim with caution.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Common mistakes that push cargo to the wrong gate</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Choosing the cheaper ocean quote while ignoring the inland leg that follows discharge.</li>
        <li>Assuming Jeddah is the natural Riyadh gateway because it is a larger Red Sea port.</li>
        <li>Forgetting the Dammam → Riyadh Dry Port handover point and clearing location.</li>
        <li>Deciding the port before the final consignee or final destination is confirmed.</li>
        <li>Comparing a direct Jeddah call against a transhipped Dammam service as if they were equal.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose Jeddah when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The consignee is in Jeddah, Makkah, Madinah, Tabuk or the western region.</li>
            <li>The cargo serves pilgrimage, religious or Red Sea trade flows.</li>
            <li>A shorter western inland leg beats a cross-country rail/road move.</li>
            <li>You are comparing direct vs transhipped Red Sea service options.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose Dammam instead when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The consignee is in Dammam, Khobar, Jubail or the Eastern Province.</li>
            <li>The cargo is oil &amp; gas, industrial, heavy or project freight.</li>
            <li>The final destination is Riyadh and the Riyadh Dry Port corridor applies.</li>
            <li>The eastern Arabian Gulf routing better matches your origin or carrier.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

