  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost differences: where Jeddah and Dammam actually diverge</h2>
      <p>
        The most important cost insight is that the <em>taxes</em> do not diverge. Import duty at 5%
        of CIF value, import VAT at 15%, excise rates and the SABER conformity fees follow the same
        national rules at both gates. What actually differs is the destination and inland stack: THC
        and terminal charges at each port, clearance and brokerage, demurrage/detention exposure, and
        — above all — the inland leg to the consignee.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why “Jeddah is cheaper than Dammam” is usually a misleading half-answer. A Jeddah
        quote that ends at the port is not comparable with a Dammam quote that includes the
        rail/road leg to Riyadh Dry Port. Always request an all-in quote that names the final place
        of delivery, then compare the same scope.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'jeddah', label: 'Jeddah' },
          { key: 'dammam', label: 'Dammam' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="Only VAT (15%) and baseline duty (5% CIF) are verified. Unquantified fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish specific amounts — request a fee schedule."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — Jeddah vs Dammam destination costs & customs" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Demurrage and detention:</strong> demurrage is charged by the terminal when the
        container stays inside the port beyond free time; detention is charged by the ocean carrier
        when the container stays outside beyond free time. Finish clearance and book the inland leg
        before discharge to keep both off the invoice.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance is the same at both gates: SABER, VAT, duty and FASAH</h2>

      <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
      <p>
        Saudi import VAT is <strong>15%</strong>, effective since 1 July 2020, and is calculated on
        the CIF value <em>plus</em> customs duty. The baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF</strong>
        under the GCC Common External Tariff, with protective or anti-dumping tariffs of 15–25%
        possible on specific goods. Excise tax adds 100% on tobacco, 50% on energy drinks and 50% on
        carbonated drinks where applicable. None of these rates change between Jeddah and Dammam.
      </p>

      <h3>SABER / SASO in the correct order</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Register the importer and product on the SABER conformity platform.</li>
        <li>Obtain the <strong>PC (Product Conformity Certificate)</strong> — product testing first, then pre-shipment inspection; validity is about one year and it is issued once per product.</li>
        <li>Once the bill of lading is available, apply for the <strong>SC (Shipment Conformity Certificate)</strong> for that specific shipment.</li>
        <li>Receive the SC <strong>before cargo arrival</strong> — since 1 January 2025, an SC applied after arrival is void and clearance fails.</li>
        <li>Submit the pre-arrival customs declaration through <strong>FASAH</strong>, Saudi Arabia’s single-window system, with the SC attached.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Documents, classification and the inland handover</h3>
      <p>
        Standard documents are the commercial invoice, bill of lading, packing list, certificate of
        origin and the SABER conformity certificate for regulated goods. Classify goods with the
        correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting, because both duty and conformity requirements
        depend on it. Note that <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States requirement and does not apply
        to Saudi Arabia; Saudi Arabia’s equivalent advance-filing step is the FASAH pre-arrival
        declaration.
      </p>
      <p>
        FASAH is the single window that connects importers, customs brokers, terminals and ZATCA, so
        the pre-arrival declaration, duty/VAT assessment and clearance status all sit in one flow. For
        Riyadh cargo, confirm whether clearance happens at Dammam or at Riyadh Dry Port, because that
        changes the document timing and the inland handover point.
      </p>

      <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Saudi customs & ports" />
  </section>

